Phase 3 verification report — Dead-letter routing (TD-2.2/TD-2.4, ADR 0010)

Scope: wrap each consumer's per-message processing branch (StockEventConsumer,
PaymentEventConsumer) in purekafka.consumer.DeadLetter, so a message that fails
processing after exhausting retries is routed to <topic>-dlq instead of
crashing/blocking the stream.

What was proven:
- A permanently-failing message (poison item id, OrderStore decorator always
  raises for it) lands on inventory-events-dlq after DeadLetter's retry budget
  is exhausted — confirmed by directly consuming the DLQ topic and asserting
  the message body mentions the poison item id.
- A healthy message produced on the same topic, same partition key, right
  after the poison one, is still processed normally (order status reaches
  Reserved) — the stream does not get stuck or killed by the poison message.
- purekafka.dlq.published metric increments (>= 1) when routing occurs.
- All existing null-safety (Phase 1) and stream-supervision (Phase 2) tests
  still pass with DeadLetter wired in.

Significant architectural finding:
Once DeadLetter wraps a consumer's processing branches, it absorbs virtually
all application-level failures via its own internal Retry *before* they ever
reach the outer StreamSupervisor. Confirmed empirically: re-running Phase 2's
original isolation test (built on a failing-once OrderStore decorator) now
shows purekafka.stream.restarts == 0, where it previously was >= 1 once
DeadLetter was layered on top. This is a net-positive real-world outcome
(fewer unnecessary whole-stream restarts for transient failures that
DeadLetter's own retry already handles), but it invalidated the original
test's premise.

Fix: rewrote KafkaConsumersSuite's Phase 2 test to use purely synthetic
Stream[IO, Unit] builds (matching purekafka's own StreamSupervisorSuite
convention) to prove the merge-isolation property directly, independent of
real consumer business logic. Added a new, separate Phase 3 test (above) to
prove DLQ routing using the real consumers end-to-end against a live Kafka
container.

Verification: sbt scalafmt scalafmtCheck test — 147 passed, 0 failed, 0
errors; scalafmtCheck clean.

Mode: reduced-prompting (automated-test verification only; no interactive
